**Interpretation.** The phrase "there is a plane α ... always results in a round disk, although the diameter may vary" only makes sense if the property holds for *every* plane (the diameter then varying with the plane). I read the hypothesis as: *for every plane, the orthogonal projection of M is a round disk.* (A single fixed plane would give one fixed projection with no "always/vary.")

---

**Proof.**

Let $K$ be the convex body bounded by $M$ (the closure of the interior). Since $M$ is a smooth 2‑manifold with convex, simply‑connected interior, $K$ is a smooth convex body with $\partial K = M$.

For a unit vector $u$, let $P_u$ be the orthogonal projection onto $u^\perp$. We have $P_u(M)=P_u(K)$: every point of $K$ lies on a segment perpendicular to $u^\perp$ joining two boundary points of $K$, and all three project to the same point. By hypothesis $P_u(K)$ is a closed round disk
$$P_u(K)=D(c(u),r(u))=\{x\in u^\perp:\ |x-c(u)|\le r(u)\},\qquad c(u)\in u^\perp,\ r(u)>0.$$

Let $h(v)=h_K(v)=\max_{x\in K}v\cdot x$, $v\in S^2$, be the support function. The support function of $P_u(K)$ in a direction $w\in S^2\cap u^\perp$ equals $h(w)$; for a disk $D(c,r)$ it equals $c\cdot w+r$. Hence
$$h(w)=c(u)\cdot w+r(u),\qquad \forall\,w\in S^2,\ w\perp u. \tag{$\star$}$$
So $h(w)+h(-w)=2r(u)$ and $h(w)-h(-w)=2c(u)\cdot w$ for $w\perp u$.

Define the even and odd parts
$$g(v)=\tfrac12(h(v)+h(-v))\;(=\text{half-width in direction }v),\qquad f(v)=\tfrac12(h(v)-h(-v)).$$
Then for $w\perp u$:
$$g(w)=r(u),\qquad f(w)=c(u)\cdot w. \tag{$\star\star$}$$

**Step 1 — $g$ is constant (constant width).** $g$ is even and continuous on $S^2$, and for each $u$ it is constant ($=r(u)$) on the great circle $u^\perp$. Fix $v\in S^2$. The point $v$ lies on $u^\perp$ for every $u\perp v$, so $g(v)=r(u)$ for all $u\perp v$; i.e. $r$ is constant on the great circle $v^\perp$. Since $v$ is arbitrary, $r$ is constant on every great circle, hence $r\equiv R$ on $S^2$. Thus $g\equiv R$, and $K$ has constant width $2R$.

**Step 2 — $f$ is a degree‑1 spherical harmonic.** $f$ is odd and smooth on $S^2$. For each $u$, along the great circle $C_u=u^\perp$ (parametrized by arc length $s$), $(\star\star)$ gives $f(w(s))=c(u)\cdot w(s)$ with $w(s)=\cos s\,a+\sin s\,b$ for orthonormal $a,b\perp u$. Hence
$$\frac{d^2 f}{ds^2}=-f \quad\text{along every great circle }C_u. \tag{$\dagger$}$$

Now compute the Laplace–Beltrami operator at any $p\in S^2$. For an orthonormal basis $e_1,e_2$ of $T_pS^2$,
$$\Delta_{S^2}f(p)=\operatorname{Hess}_{S^2}f(e_1,e_1)+\operatorname{Hess}_{S^2}f(e_2,e_2)=\frac{d^2}{ds^2}f\big|_{\gamma_1}+\frac{d^2}{ds^2}f\big|_{\gamma_2},$$
where $\gamma_i$ is the unit‑speed geodesic (great circle) through $p$ with $\gamma_i'(0)=e_i$. Every great circle is some $C_u$, so by $(\dagger)$ each term equals $-f(p)$. Therefore
$$\Delta_{S^2}f=-2f.$$
The eigenvalues of $\Delta_{S^2}$ on $S^2$ are $-l(l+1)$, $l=0,1,2,\dots$; $-2=-1\cdot2$ gives $l=1$. Thus $f$ is a degree‑1 spherical harmonic: there exists $t\in\mathbb R^3$ with
$$f(v)=t\cdot v,\qquad v\in S^2.$$

**Step 3 — $K$ is a ball.** Combining the two steps,
$$h(v)=g(v)+f(v)=R+t\cdot v,\qquad v\in S^2.$$
This is precisely the support function of the closed ball $\overline{B}(t,R)$ of radius $R$ centered at $t$. A convex body is uniquely determined by its support function, so $K=\overline{B}(t,R)$. Consequently
$$M=\partial K=\partial\overline{B}(t,R),$$
which is a round sphere.

**Conclusion.** Yes — under the stated hypothesis $M$ is necessarily a sphere. $\blacksquare$

